== Much Time in Religion

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[IX. It is no certain sign that the religious affections persons have are such as have in them the nature of true religion, or that they do not, that they incline persons to spend much time in religion and to be zealously engaged in the external duties of worship.]

Lately this has very unreasonably been looked on as an argument against the religious affections some have had, that they spend so much time in reading, praying, singing, hearing sermons, and the like. It is plain from the Scripture that it is the tendency of true grace to make persons delight very much in such religious exercises. True grace had this effect on #emph[Anna] the prophetess: “She never left the temple, but worshiped night and day, fasting and praying” (Luke 2:37). And grace had this effect on the early Christians in #emph[Jerusalem:] “With one accord they continued to meet daily in the temple courts and to break bread from house to house, sharing their meals with gladness and sincerity of heart, praising God” (Acts 2:46–47). Grace made #emph[Daniel] delight in the duty of prayer and solemnly attend to it three times a day. It did the same for #emph[David:] “Morning, noon, and night, I will pray” (Ps 55:17). Grace makes the saints delight in singing praises to God: “Sing praises to His name, for it is pleasant” (Ps 135:3). And in Ps 147:1: “Hallelujah! How good it is to sing praises to our God, how pleasant and lovely to praise Him!” It also makes them delight to hear the word of God preached. It makes the gospel a joyful sound to them (Ps 89:15), and makes the feet of those who publish this good news beautiful: “How beautiful on the mountains are the feet of those who bring good news,” and so on! (Isa 52:7). It makes them love God’s public worship: “O LORD, I love the house where You dwell, the place where Your glory resides” (Ps 26:8). And in Ps 27:4: “One thing I have asked of the LORD; this is what I desire: to dwell in the house of the LORD all the days of my life, to gaze on the beauty of the LORD and seek Him in His temple.” And in Ps 84:1–2 and following: “How lovely is Your dwelling place, O LORD of Hosts! My soul longs, even faints, for the courts of the LORD. … Even the sparrow has found a home, and the swallow a nest for herself, where she places her young near Your altars, O LORD of Hosts, my King and my God. How blessed are those who dwell in Your house! They are ever praising You. Blessed are those … whose hearts are set on pilgrimage. As they pass through the Valley of Baca … they go from strength to strength, until each appears before God in Zion.” And verse 10: “Better is one day in Your courts than a thousand elsewhere.”

This is the nature of true grace. But on the other hand, persons being inclined to abound and be zealously engaged in the external exercises of religion, and to spend much time in them, is no sure evidence of grace, because such an inclination is found in many who have no grace. So it was with the #emph[Israelites] of old, whose services were abominable to God. They kept the #emph[New Moons and Sabbaths and convocations, and spread out their hands, and multiplied their prayers] (Isa 1:12–15). So it was with the #emph[Pharisees.] They #emph[made long prayers and fasted twice a week.] False religion may make persons loud and earnest in prayer: “You#super[+] cannot fast as you#super[+] do today to make your#super[+] voice heard on high” (Isa 58:4). Religion which is not spiritual and saving may make men delight in religious duties and ordinances: “For day after day they seek Me and delight to know My ways, like a nation that does what is right and does not forsake the ordinance of their God. They ask Me for righteous ordinances; they delight in drawing near to God” (Isa 58:2). It may make them delight in hearing the word of God preached, as it was with #emph[Ezekiel’s] hearers: “They come to you as My people come, and sit before you as My people, and hear your words; but they do not put them into practice. Although they express love with their mouths, their hearts pursue dishonest gain. Indeed, you are to them like a singer of love songs with a beautiful voice, who skillfully plays an instrument. They hear your words but do not put them into practice” (Ezek 33:31–32). So it was with #emph[Herod.] He #emph[listened] to John the Baptist #emph[gladly] (Mark 6:20). So it was with others of John’s hearers: #emph[for a season they rejoiced in his light] (John 5:35). So the hearers on stony ground #emph[heard the word with joy.]

Experience shows that persons, from false religion, may be inclined to be exceedingly abundant in the external exercises of religion, indeed to give themselves up to them and devote almost their whole time to them. In former times a sort of people called #emph[recluses] were very numerous in the #emph[Roman] church. They forsook the world and utterly abandoned the company of mankind, and shut themselves up close in a narrow cell, with a vow never to stir out of it nor to see the face of any human being again (except that they might be visited in case of sickness), to spend all their days in the exercises of devotion and communion with God. There were also great multitudes in ancient times called #emph[hermits] and #emph[anchorites,] who left the world to spend all their days in lonely deserts, to give themselves up to religious contemplation and exercises of devotion. Some sorts of them had no dwellings but the caves and hollows of the mountains, and no food but what the earth produced of itself. I once lived for many months next door to a #emph[Jew] (the houses joined one to another), and I had much opportunity to observe him daily. He seemed to me the most devout person I ever saw in my life. A great part of his time was spent in acts of devotion at his eastern window, which opened next to mine, and he seemed to be most earnestly engaged, not only in the daytime, but at times whole nights.
